description:
FILED s543b (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-21, after the rebase onto 22ec674b) -- found by the coordinator's composition probe for #4794
(an `undef $x;` as the FIRST statement of a sub body, on its own line).  A COVERAGE HOLE of #4794's flush, not a regression
(main 78202d0a is wrong the same way) and not a line-tracking interaction (the same with PCL_OPT=-line-track).

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 prints `a 5 / b 7 / c 9 / d 10`; PCL on main 78202d0a AND on s543b prints `a 0 / b 0 / c 0 / d 0`):

    use strict; use warnings;
    my $f = "/tmp/pcl-flushfirst-$$.out";
    sub mk { open my $fh, ">", $f or die; $fh }
    sub size { -s $f // 0 }
    my $g = mk();
    sub drop_g {
      undef $g;
      print "a ", size(), "\n";
    }
    print $g "first"; drop_g();
    my $h = mk();
    sub drop_h {
      $h = undef;
      print "b ", size(), "\n";
    }
    print $h "second!"; drop_h();
    my $k = mk();
    sub drop_k {
      undef($k);
    }
    print $k "third...."; drop_k(); print "c ", size(), "\n";
    unlink $f;

(s543b worktree scratch/s543b/p8/flush-first.pl.)  perl: the sub drops the LAST reference to the handle, so it is closed and
its bytes are in the file; PCL writes nothing until a trigger or exit (ns-escaping-filehandle).

WHICH SHAPES GET THE FLUSH (s543b tree, p8/shapes.pl, emission grep): a sub-LOCAL `my $fh = mk(); ...; undef $fh;` -- yes;
`$fh = undef;` on a sub-local -- yes; a file-level `undef $g;` and one inside a file-level bare block -- yes; `undef $g;` /
`$g = undef;` / `undef($g);` INSIDE A SUB on a file-level lexical the sub CAPTURES -- NO (first statement or later).

WHY.  Parser2::_fh_drop_flush_name -> _fh_drop_stmt_name answers only for a name that is a key of `$vi`, the VarAnnotator
table of the unit being lowered; a sub body's table holds the sub's own lexicals, so a captured outer `my` is not a key and
the `(p-flush-dropped-handle $g)` is never emitted.  The shape reader is fine (it reads the statement).

WHAT A FIX NEEDS (not taken: outside s543b's re-take list): the predicate "this name is a lexical SCALAR" answered for a
captured binding too -- by the ONE resolver (Binder: the name's binding at the statement is a `my`/`state` scalar, wherever
it was declared), not a second scope walk (parser-review-s525 standing rule).  The emitted form already works on a captured
variable (`$g` in the sub body is the shared cell; p-flush-dropped-handle peeks the slot without FETCH).  Guard: the
reproducer above as a row in Pl/t/fh-collect-01.t (row 7's family).  The same hole probably holds for `undef $obj->{fh}`-free
shapes only; the hash-element / object shapes stay out of #4794's scope as before.
